\ Spitfire: the scenery that stays put. The coasts, lochs and rivers come
\ from geography.telic; the ground over Scotland is scotland-terrain.telic's
\ surveyed heights every 800 m, which round the summits down (Ben Lomond's
\ 974 m to 778), with cones for the hills too narrow for that grid: Arthur's
\ Seat, Calton Hill, the castle rocks of Edinburgh, Stirling and Dumbarton,
\ the Abbey Craig, North Berwick Law, Craig Phadrig and the Forth's islands.
\ The towns and airfields stand on level ground at sea level, the surveyed
\ ground rising or falling to it over 2 km round them. The towns are their landmarks at the
\ map's positions, built to their published heights where a figure exists
\ (the Forth Bridge's towers 110 m above high water and its rail 46 m, the
\ Scott Monument 61 m, the University's tower 85 m), with streets of
\ tenements, shops and New Town terraces between them; the airfields are the
\ RAF stations of 1940: Turnhouse and Drem for Edinburgh, Abbotsinch for
\ Glasgow, Dalcross for Inverness, Dyce for Aberdeen, Montrose, Wick and
\ Castletown in Caithness, Hatston and Skeabrae in Orkney, Sumburgh in
\ Shetland. Over Orkney and Shetland the ground is scotland-terrain.telic's
\ second grid. Round the Trondheimsfjord the ground is
\ norway-terrain.telic's surveyed heights, round Narvik and Bardufoss
\ narvik.telic's, filled in the colours of its land cover by height and
\ slope, farmland, forest, heath, rock and snow, and taken as level where
\ the scenery stands on it. Everything is built once
\ into objects and gathered into the collection the view culls.

0x6a9ad0 constant bank-colour
0x7c9a5a constant hill-colour
0xc8c0b0 constant stone-colour
0xa09080 constant town-colour
0xd04030 constant bridge-colour
0xe8e8e0 constant runway-colour
0x90a070 constant field-colour
0x5a8040 constant tree-colour

variable scenery
[ ] to scenery
\ every hill as [ x z radius height ], for the ground's height under the aircraft
variable hill-cones
[ ] to hill-cones

\ the places where the ground is taken as level at a height, the scenery standing on it, each [ x z radius height ]. In Scotland, where the scenery is built at sea level, the towns and airfields at 0 out to the reach the countryside leaves clear of fields, Aberdeen within 2,500 m of Marischal College, so Old Aberdeen is in it, Dyce at its height, 66 m, Dumbarton's rock and the Abbey Craig under their cones; in Norway Trondheim within 2,500 m of Nidaros, Værnes within 1,500 m and Munkholmen within 400 m at sea level, Narvik's centre within 800 m at sea level, Bardufoss within 1,500 m at its height
[ [ :edinburgh-castle 3500 0 ] [ :leith 1500 0 ] [ :glasgow-cathedral 4500 0 ] [ :govan 2000 0 ] [ :clydebank 1500 0 ]
  [ :inverness-castle 2000 0 ] [ :stirling-castle 1500 0 ] [ :dumbarton-castle 600 0 ] [ :wallace-monument 500 0 ]
  [ :turnhouse 1200 0 ] [ :drem 1200 0 ] [ :abbotsinch 1200 0 ] [ :dalcross 1200 0 ] [ :wick 1200 0 ]
  [ :marischal-college 2500 0 ] [ :peterhead 1000 0 ] [ :montrose 1000 0 ] [ :kirkwall 1000 0 ] [ :lerwick 800 0 ]
  [ :hatston 1200 0 ] [ :skeabrae 1200 0 ] [ :castletown 1200 0 ] [ :sumburgh 1200 0 ] [ :montrose-air-station 1200 0 ]
  [ :dyce 1200 landmarks :dyce @ 2 @i ]
  [ :nidaros 2500 0 ] [ :vaernes 1500 0 ] [ :munkholmen 400 0 ] [ :narvik 800 0 ] [ :bardufoss 1500 landmarks :bardufoss @ 2 @i ] ] ( place |
	place spread to height to radius to key
	landmarks key @ to point
	[ point first point second radius height ]
) map constant level-places

\ the surveyed grids, each a frame of its south-west corner, spacing, size and heights, and the grid steps a far tile's triangles span: over Scotland and over Orkney and Shetland 2, 1.6 km; round the Trondheimsfjord 4, 1.6 km; round Narvik and Bardufoss 2, 800 m, so the narrower fjords stay open at a distance
{ :west scotland-terrain-west :south scotland-terrain-south :spacing scotland-terrain-spacing :columns scotland-terrain-columns
  :rows scotland-terrain-rows :heights scotland-terrain-heights :far-step 2 } constant scotland-grid
{ :west isles-terrain-west :south isles-terrain-south :spacing isles-terrain-spacing :columns isles-terrain-columns
  :rows isles-terrain-rows :heights isles-terrain-heights :far-step 2 } constant isles-grid
{ :west terrain-west :south terrain-south :spacing terrain-spacing :columns terrain-columns :rows terrain-rows :heights terrain-heights
  :far-step 4 :land-cover :trondelag } constant trondheim-grid
{ :west narvik-terrain-west :south narvik-terrain-south :spacing narvik-terrain-spacing :columns narvik-terrain-columns
  :rows narvik-terrain-rows :heights narvik-terrain-heights :far-step 2 :land-cover :ofoten } constant narvik-grid
[ scotland-grid isles-grid trondheim-grid narvik-grid ] constant terrain-grids

\ whether a point lies within a grid or a margin outside it
: near-grid? ( x z grid margin -- flag )
	| x z grid margin |
	x grid@west margin - > x grid@west grid@columns grid@spacing * + margin + < and
	z grid@south margin - > and z grid@south grid@rows grid@spacing * + margin + < and ;

\ === Norway's land cover ===

\ the land cover of Norway's grids, as aerial photographs of Trøndelag and Ofoten show it (colours and heights estimates read from the photographs; Narvik's tree line below the mountain plateau's 650 m, ut.no): farmland on the level ground below 150 m; above it spruce in Trøndelag, nearly black-green, to 700 m, birch round Narvik, lighter, to 450 m; heath for 150 m above the trees, then bare rock; snow above the snow line on all but the steepest faces, the spruce keeping most of its dark under it
150 constant farmland-height
0.99 constant farmland-upright
0.6 constant snow-upright
0x5e7c3c constant farmland-colour
0x22331e constant spruce-colour
0x4a6e30 constant birch-colour
0x6a6450 constant heath-colour
0x787268 constant rock-colour
0xe6eaee constant snow-colour
0.15 constant snow-on-forest
100000 constant no-snow

\ the height the snow lies down to, set by each flight
variable snow-line
no-snow to snow-line

\ the land cover of the grid a point lies in, :trondelag or :ofoten, or null outside Norway's grids
: land-cover-at ( x z -- cover )
	| x z |
	terrain-grids ( grid | x z grid 0 near-grid? grid :land-cover null @or null? not and ) filter to holding
	holding size 0 = if null else holding first :land-cover @ then ;

\ the ground's colour in a land cover at a height, its uprightness the cosine of its slope: farmland, forest, heath or rock by height and slope, whitened above the snow line
: cover-colour ( cover height upright -- colour )
	| cover height upright |
	cover :trondelag = if spruce-colour 700 else birch-colour 450 then to tree-line to forest
	height farmland-height < upright farmland-upright > and if farmland-colour to colour false to wooded
	else height tree-line < if forest to colour true to wooded
	else height tree-line 150 + < if heath-colour else rock-colour then to colour false to wooded then then
	height snow-line < upright snow-upright < or if colour exit then
	wooded if snow-colour colour snow-on-forest blended else snow-colour then ;

\ the level ground's colour for a point of view: the ground's, or over Norway's grids the land cover's on level ground at sea level
: use-level-ground ( position -- )
	| position ^level-ground-colour |
	position first position 2 @i land-cover-at to cover
	cover null? if ground-colour else cover 0 1 cover-colour monochrome if grey-of then then to level-ground-colour ;

\ the height at a point in one of the grids: the sea where the nearest corner is below 2 m, so the sea stays level up to the shore within half a corner's spacing; elsewhere between the four nearest corners; null outside the grid
: grid-height ( x z west south spacing columns rows heights -- height )
	| x z west south spacing columns rows heights |
	x west - spacing / to across
	z south - spacing / to along
	across 0 < along 0 < or across columns 1- >= or along rows 1- >= or if null exit then
	across round-down to column
	along round-down to row
	across column - to east-share
	along row - to north-share
	heights along round across round @i,j 2 < if 0 exit then
	heights row column @i,j 1 east-share - * heights row column 1+ @i,j east-share * + 1 north-share - *
	heights row 1+ column @i,j 1 east-share - * heights row 1+ column 1+ @i,j east-share * + north-share * + ;

\ the level place a point lies in, or null
: level-place-at ( x z -- place )
	| x z |
	level-places ( place | place first x - sq place second z - sq + place 2 @i sq < ) filter to levels
	levels size 0 > if levels first else null then ;

\ the ground's height as surveyed: the 50 m grid round Fættenfjord where it reaches, else the first grid holding the point; 0 outside them
: surveyed-height ( x z -- height )
	| x z |
	x z fine-terrain-west fine-terrain-south fine-terrain-spacing fine-terrain-count fine-terrain-count fine-terrain-heights grid-height to fine
	fine null? not if fine exit then
	0 terrain-grids size 1 do k
		terrain-grids k @i to grid
		x z grid@west grid@south grid@spacing grid@columns grid@rows grid@heights grid-height to coarse
		coarse null? not if coarse exit then
	loop
	0 ;

\ the metres outside a level place over which the ground rises or falls from its height to the surveyed
2000 constant level-ramp

\ the ground's height: a level place's height within it; across the ramp round it the level's height blended into the surveyed, the nearer the more; elsewhere the surveyed
: terrain-height ( x z -- height )
	| x z |
	0 to level-share
	0 to level-height
	0 level-places size 1 do k
		level-places k @i to place
		place first x - sq place second z - sq + sqrt place 2 @i - level-ramp / 1 swap - 0 1 clamp to share
		share level-share > if
			share to level-share
			place 3 @i to level-height
		then
	loop
	level-share 1 = if level-height exit then
	x z surveyed-height to surveyed
	level-height surveyed - level-share * surveyed + ;

\ the ground's height at a point under a set of hill cones: the tallest cone over it, or the terrain, else sea level
: ground-height-under ( x z cones -- height )
	| x z cones |
	cones x z terrain-height ( highest cone |
		cone spread to peak to reach to cone-z to cone-x
		cone-x x - sq cone-z z - sq + sqrt reach / 1 swap - peak * highest max2
	) reduce ;

\ the ground's height at a point
: ground-height ( x z -- height )
	| x z |
	x z hill-cones ground-height-under ;

\ === the hills filled ===

\ the share of a hillside's light it keeps facing away from the sun (an estimate), the rest falling with the cosine of its slope to the sun
0.55 constant hill-shadow

\ a mesh of filled triangles from a list of points [ x y z ] and a list of triangles [ a b c ] of their indices: the points as rows [ x y z 1 ], the corners as three arrays, the triangles' centres as rows; for each triangle its shade from its slope facing the sun, its mean height, that height's share of 900 m and its uprightness, the cosine of its slope; colours cached per palette and haze; null when there are no triangles
: filled-mesh ( points triangles -- mesh )
	| points triangles |
	triangles size 0 = if null exit then
	points ( point | point spread 1 4 array ) map flatten points size 4 matrix to point-rows
	triangles ( triangle |
		triangle spread to c to b to a
		points a @i points b @i v+ points c @i v+ 1 3 / v* spread 1 4 array
	) map flatten triangles size 4 matrix to centre-rows
	triangles ( triangle |
		triangle spread to c to b to a
		points b @i points a @i v- points c @i points a @i v- v-cross to normal
		normal second 0 < if normal -1 v* to normal then
		normal v-length 1e-9 max2 to length
		normal sun-direction v-dot length / 0 max2 1 hill-shadow - * hill-shadow +
	) map to shades
	triangles ( triangle |
		triangle spread to c to b to a
		points b @i points a @i v- points c @i points a @i v- v-cross to normal
		normal second abs normal v-length 1e-9 max2 /
	) map to uprights
	triangles ( triangle | triangle ( corner | points corner @i second ) map 0 ' + reduce 3 / ) map to heights
	heights ( height | height 900 / 0 1 clamp ) map to fractions
	{ :points point-rows :centroids centre-rows
	  :first-corners triangles ( triangle | triangle first ) map
	  :second-corners triangles ( triangle | triangle second ) map
	  :third-corners triangles ( triangle | triangle 2 @i ) map
	  :shades shades :fractions fractions :heights heights :uprights uprights :colours { } } ;

\ a mesh's triangles' colours at a haze, a share of their light from 0 to 1 in sixteenths: the ground's colour lightened toward the hilltops', or in Norway its land cover's, darkened by each slope's shade, faded into the sky; grey in a photograph; made once for each palette, snow line and haze and kept
: mesh-colours ( mesh haze -- colours )
	| mesh haze |
	haze 16 * round to level
	mesh :land-cover null @or to cover
	sky-colour ground-colour hill-top-colour level monochrome cover snow-line "{0}/{1}/{2}/{3}/{4}/{5}/{6}" format string>symbol to key
	mesh@colours key has? if mesh@colours key @ exit then
	level 16 / to light
	[ 0 mesh@shades size 1 do k
		cover null? if hill-top-colour ground-colour mesh@fractions k @i blended
		else cover mesh@heights k @i mesh@uprights k @i cover-colour then
		mesh@shades k @i dimmed
		sky-colour light blended
		monochrome if grey-of then
	loop ] to colours
	mesh@colours colours key ! drop
	colours ;

\ the filled ground: the surveyed terrain's tiles, each 8 grid steps square, and the hills kept as cones, each [ :centre :radius :near :far ]; their spheres for culling, gathered once the scenery is built
variable terrain-tiles
[ ] to terrain-tiles
variable terrain-tile-centres
0 4 0-matrix to terrain-tile-centres
variable terrain-tile-radii
0 1 0-matrix to terrain-tile-radii
\ within this distance a tile is drawn by its near mesh, beyond by its far one
6000 constant terrain-near

\ a hill filled: its foot, its shoulder and its summit joined by triangles, two between the rings for each side, one from the shoulder to the summit
: hill-mesh ( centre radius height seed -- mesh )
	| centre radius height seed |
	centre radius height seed hill-rings to summit to shoulder to foot
	foot size to n-sides
	foot shoulder concat [ summit ] concat to points
	[ 0 n-sides 1 do k
		k 1+ n-sides mod to next
		[ k next next n-sides + ]
		[ k next n-sides + k n-sides + ]
		[ k n-sides + next n-sides + n-sides 2 * ]
	loop ] to triangles
	points triangles filled-mesh ;

\ the filled ground's spheres gathered for culling, once every tile and hill is in
: gather-terrain ( -- )
	| ^terrain-tile-centres ^terrain-tile-radii |
	terrain-tiles ( tile | tile@centre spread 1 4 array ) map flatten terrain-tiles size 4 matrix to terrain-tile-centres
	terrain-tiles ( tile | tile@radius ) map vector to terrain-tile-radii ;

\ the water's rings, gathered as the scenery is built: the map's frame, the land's outlines, the lochs, the islands, the rivers' strips across the land and Leith's wet docks; with them, as the mainland's region, Orkney and Shetland coarsely, and the islands in detail with the mainland's outlines as a region of their own, the nearer of the two in use, so Glasgow's frames do not pay for Shetland's coast; the Trondheimsfjord's region with the Nidelva through the town
variable water-rings
[ ] to water-rings

\ add a ring to the water's
: keep-water-ring ( ring -- )
	| ring ^water-rings |
	water-rings ring add-last! drop ;

\ add a finished sketch to the scenery
: keep ( sketch colour -- )
	| sketch colour ^scenery |
	scenery sketch colour sketch>object add-last! drop ;

\ add a finished sketch of separate solids whose every line lies on a face to the scenery: without its list of lines, which only a wireframe reads, so the collector need not walk it, and drawn without the depth test, the far-to-near order being enough for solids that do not cross
: keep-solid ( sketch colour -- )
	| sketch colour ^scenery |
	scenery sketch colour sketch>object [ ] :edges ! false :depth-test ! add-last! drop ;

\ a landmark's ground point, [ x 0 z ]
: landmark-ground ( key -- point )
	| key |
	landmarks key @ spread drop to z to x
	[ x 0 z ] ;

\ a landmark's height from the map
: landmark-height ( key -- metres ) landmarks swap @ 2 @i ;

\ the heading from one landmark to another, degrees
: landmark-heading ( from to -- degrees )
	| origin destination |
	destination landmark-ground origin landmark-ground v- heading-of ;

\ === lines on the ground ===

\ a path as objects of at most twelve points each, so each piece culls on its own
: keep-path ( points colour -- )
	| points colour |
	0 points size 1- 11 do start
		points start skip 12 take to piece
		piece size 2 >= if
			new-sketch to sketch
			sketch piece sketch-path
			sketch colour keep
		then
	loop ;

\ a stretch of river as a ring of [ x z ] points: one bank downstream, the other back up
: river-ring ( course half-width -- ring )
	| course half-width |
	course half-width offset-path course half-width negate offset-path reverse concat
	( point | [ point first point 2 @i ] ) map ;

\ a river as its strips of water where its course crosses land: runs of the segments whose middles are not already water, as wide as the river
: keep-river ( course half-width -- )
	| course half-width |
	[ ] to run
	0 course size 1- 1 do k
		course k @i to start
		course k 1+ @i to finish
		start first finish first + 2 / start second finish second + 2 / water-at? if
			run size 2 >= if run half-width river-ring keep-water-ring then
			[ ] to run
		else
			run size 0 = if run start add-last! drop then
			run finish add-last! drop
		then
	loop
	run size 2 >= if run half-width river-ring keep-water-ring then ;

\ an island too small for the map's land: a ring of eight points round its landmark, as land amid the water
: keep-island ( key radius -- )
	| key radius |
	key landmark-ground to centre
	[ 0 8 1 do k
		k 8 / TAU * to angle
		[ centre first angle sin radius * + centre 2 @i angle cos radius * + ]
	loop ] keep-water-ring ;

: build-waters ( -- )
	| ^water-rings |
	land-outlines [ south-land ] concat to water-rings
	lochs values ( loch | loch@shores ( shore | shore keep-water-ring ) each ) each
	water-rings water-rings!
	leith-wet-docks ( dock | dock outline-middle spread water-at? not if dock keep-water-ring then ) each
	rivers@clyde 90 keep-river
	rivers@forth 70 keep-river
	rivers@kelvin 12 keep-river
	rivers@ness ( course | course 25 keep-river ) each
	[ [ :inchkeith 500 ] [ :inchcolm 300 ] [ :cramond-island 300 ] [ :bass-rock 200 ] ] ( island | island spread keep-island ) each
	:faettenfjord landmark-ground to norway-centre
	:narvik landmark-ground to narvik-centre
	[ [ 0 130000 water-rings isles-coarse-outlines concat rings>edge-set ]
	  [ 0 470000 land-outlines isles-outlines concat rings>edge-set ]
	  [ norway-centre first norway-centre 2 @i norway-outlines norway-bands concat nidelva-water concat rings>edge-set ]
	  [ narvik-centre first narvik-centre 2 @i narvik-outlines narvik-bands concat rings>edge-set ] ] water-regions!
	[ 0 0 0 ] use-water-near ;

\ === hills ===

\ a cone of ground for the ground's height alone, where a model draws the hill itself
: keep-cone ( centre radius height -- )
	| centre radius height ^hill-cones |
	hill-cones [ centre first centre 2 @i radius height ] add-last! drop ;

\ a hill at a point: its cone for the ground's height, and its filled mesh among the filled ground
: keep-hill ( centre radius height seed -- )
	| centre radius height seed |
	centre radius height keep-cone
	centre radius height seed hill-mesh to mesh
	terrain-tiles
	{ :centre centre [ 0 height 2 / 0 ] v+ :radius radius 1.3 * height max2 :near mesh :far mesh }
	add-last! drop ;

\ a tile's mesh from a corner of the grid, cells to a side, taken a step at a time: each cell two triangles, a triangle left out when any of its corners is sea, so no slope reaches out over the water; the corners' heights the ground's
: tile-mesh ( grid row column n-cells step -- mesh )
	| grid row column n-cells step |
	n-cells step / round 1+ to count
	[ 0 count 1 do r
		0 count 1 do c
			grid@west column c step * + grid@spacing * + to x
			grid@south row r step * + grid@spacing * + to z
			[ x x z terrain-height z ]
		loop
	loop ] to points
	[ 0 count 1- 1 do r
		0 count 1- 1 do c
			r count * c + to p00
			p00 1+ to p01
			p00 count + to p10
			p10 1+ to p11
			[ [ p00 p10 p11 ] [ p00 p11 p01 ] ] ( triangle |
				triangle ( corner | points corner @i second 2 < ) any? not
			) filter spread
		loop
	loop ] to triangles
	points triangles filled-mesh ;

\ the ground of one of the grids in tiles of 8 grid steps (3.2 km in Norway, 6.4 km in Scotland), its sphere for culling 6 steps across: each tile filled by triangles, a grid step's near and the grid's far step's far, in Norway each carrying its grid's land cover; a tile all sea left out
: build-grid-terrain ( grid -- )
	| grid |
	grid@west to west
	grid@south to south
	grid@spacing to spacing
	grid@columns to n-columns
	grid@rows to n-rows
	0 n-rows 1- 8 do tile-row
		0 n-columns 1- 8 do tile-column
			8 n-rows 1- tile-row - min2 to n-tile-rows
			8 n-columns 1- tile-column - min2 to n-tile-columns
			west tile-column spacing * + to tile-west
			south tile-row spacing * + to tile-south
			tile-west 8 spacing * + fine-terrain-west > tile-west fine-terrain-west fine-terrain-count fine-terrain-spacing * + < and
			tile-south 8 spacing * + fine-terrain-south > and tile-south fine-terrain-south fine-terrain-count fine-terrain-spacing * + < and
			if 0.5 else 1 then to near-step
			grid tile-row tile-column n-tile-rows n-tile-columns min2 near-step tile-mesh to near-mesh
			grid tile-row tile-column n-tile-rows n-tile-columns min2 grid@far-step tile-mesh to far-mesh
			grid :land-cover null @or to cover
			cover null? not near-mesh null? not and if near-mesh cover :land-cover ! to near-mesh then
			cover null? not far-mesh null? not and if far-mesh cover :land-cover ! to far-mesh then
			near-mesh null? not if
				west tile-column 4 + spacing * + to centre-x
				south tile-row 4 + spacing * + to centre-z
				terrain-tiles
				{ :centre [ centre-x centre-x centre-z terrain-height centre-z ] :radius spacing 6 * :near near-mesh :far far-mesh }
				add-last! drop
			then
		loop
	loop ;

\ the ground of every grid
: build-norway-terrain ( -- ) terrain-grids ( grid | grid build-grid-terrain ) each ;

\ how much farther than its centre a tile is sorted: a surveyed grid's tile, 2 km or more round, by its radius, so it is drawn before whatever stands on it; a hill's own tile not at all, so a hill hides what lies behind it
: ground-tile-reach ( tile -- metres ) | tile | tile@radius 2000 >= if tile@radius else 0 then ;

\ the visible tiles as items to draw in order of depth, [ depth :terrain tile ], a grid's tile by its far side
: terrain-items ( -- items )
	terrain-tiles size 0 = if [ ] exit then
	terrain-tile-centres terrain-tile-radii visible-objects to shown
	shown num-elements 0 = if [ ] exit then
	terrain-tile-centres camera matmul 2 @j to depths
	[ 0 shown num-elements 1 do k
		shown k @e to index
		terrain-tiles index @i to tile
		[ depths index @e tile ground-tile-reach + :terrain tile ]
	loop ] ;

\ one tile: its near or far mesh by its distance, its colours at its haze
: draw-terrain-tile ( tile -- )
	| tile |
	tile@centre eye@position v- v-length to distance
	distance terrain-near < if tile@near else tile@far then to mesh
	mesh null? not if
		mesh mesh distance depth-light mesh-colours draw-filled-mesh
	then ;

: build-hills ( -- )
	:arthurs-seat landmark-ground 700 :arthurs-seat landmark-height 1 keep-hill
	:calton-hill landmark-ground 250 :calton-hill landmark-height 2 keep-hill
	:st-margarets-chapel landmark-ground 160 78 keep-cone
	:north-berwick-law landmark-ground 400 :north-berwick-law landmark-height 7 keep-hill
	:bass-rock landmark-ground 200 :bass-rock landmark-height 8 keep-hill
	:craig-phadrig landmark-ground 600 172 9 keep-hill
	:inchkeith landmark-ground 500 60 10 keep-hill
	:inchcolm landmark-ground 300 30 11 keep-hill
	:cramond-island landmark-ground 300 :cramond-island landmark-height 12 keep-hill
	:stirling-castle landmark-ground 300 75 13 keep-hill
	:wallace-monument landmark-ground 400 90 14 keep-hill
	:dumbarton-castle landmark-ground 200 73 15 keep-hill ;

\ === airfields ===

\ an RAF airfield of 1940: the landing ground filled to its boundary, a runway painted on it with dashes down it, hangars and the watch office, beside a point on a heading
: keep-airfield ( centre heading -- )
	| centre heading |
	new-sketch to field
	field centre heading 1600 1600 0 rectangle-corners sketch-floor
	field centre heading 46 1250 0 rectangle-corners centre [ 0 1 0 ] v- runway-colour sketch-painted-plate
	field field-colour keep
	new-sketch to runway
	-560 561 80 do along
		runway [ centre heading 0 0.1 along placed centre heading 0 0.1 along 40 + placed ] sketch-path
	loop
	runway runway-colour keep
	new-sketch to hangars
	[ -260 -130 0 ] ( along | hangars centre heading 520 0 along placed heading 40 60 12 15 sketch-house ) each
	hangars centre heading 470 0 160 placed heading 14 10 8 sketch-box
	hangars centre heading 470 0 230 placed 0 3 3 10 sketch-box
	hangars town-colour keep ;

\ the airfields on their headings: Dyce on today's 16/34 and Sumburgh on today's 09/27, both at their Wikidata points; the other headings estimates
: build-airfields ( -- )
	:turnhouse landmark-ground 60 keep-airfield
	:drem landmark-ground 90 keep-airfield
	:abbotsinch landmark-ground 50 keep-airfield
